    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1">

                <pb facs="0015" n="1" sameAs="frus:frus1947v06/pg1"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">845C.00/1–1447: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in the United Kingdom</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Gallman</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1947-01-14T13:00:00+00:00">January 14, 1947—1
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>246. 1. Both Laithwaite<anchor n="2" xml:id="d1-f1"/> and Smith<anchor n="3"
                        xml:id="d1-f2"/> expressed fervent hopes last night at impressive British
                    Govt reception for Burmese leaders that talks with Burmese leaders will be
                    successful. Smith said that at second meeting yesterday British suggested
                    division of work among committees. (Emb’s 228, January 13.<anchor n="4"
                        xml:id="d1-f3"/>) Burmese replied that they preferred to operate as single
                    group, implication being, according to Smith, that members Burmese delegation
                    desire to watch each other.</p>
                <p>2. Aung San<anchor n="5" xml:id="d1-f4"/> and Tin Tut<anchor n="6" xml:id="d1-f5"
                    /> spoke cordially of Packer<anchor n="7" xml:id="d1-f6"/> and of interest in
                    Burmese problems. They promised to get in touch with Embassy when talks are
                    further advanced.</p>
                <p>3. Dalton, Chancellor of Exchequer, was overheard to say to Aung San that he
                    should come along to British Treasury for talks “after everything else has been
                    decided”.</p>
                <p>4. Temperate character Aung San’s response to Attlee’s<anchor n="8"
                        xml:id="d1-f7"/> speech of welcome pleased both Laithwaite and Smith.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gallman</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1-f1" n="2" xml:id="d1-f1-content">
                <p>Sir Gilbert Laithwaite, British Deputy Under Secretary of State for Burma.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1-f2" n="3" xml:id="d1-f2-content">
                <p>F. W. H. Smith, British Acting Assistant Under Secretary of State for Burma.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1-f3" n="4" xml:id="d1-f3-content">
                <p>Not printed; it reported that the first meeting was “purely formal.”
                    (845C.00/1–1347)</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1-f4" n="5" xml:id="d1-f4-content">
                <p>Head of the Burma Mission to London and Deputy Chairman of the Burmese Executive
                    Council; also Counselor for Defense and External Affairs and President of the
                    Anti-Fascist People’s Freedom League (AFPFL)
                    of Burma.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1-f5" n="6" xml:id="d1-f5-content">
                <p>Counselor for Finance of the Burmese Executive Council and member of the Burma
                    Mission to London.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1-f6" n="7" xml:id="d1-f6-content">
                <p>Earl L. Packer, Consul General at Rangoon.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1-f7" n="8" xml:id="d1-f7-content">
                <p>Clement R. Attlee, British Prime Minister.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
